title: Your first project
section: Introduction
desc: Grow the Getting started project with data files, a dependency, a group, parallel workers, a profile and run history.
lede: You have a project with one feature. In ten minutes you will add test data from a file, make one scenario depend on another, run in parallel and read the plan.
---
<p>This page continues from <a href="getting-started.html#first-project">Getting started</a>. Every file below was run as shown.</p>

<h2>1. Test data from a file</h2>
<p>Put the rows in a CSV file instead of an Examples table.</p>
<cb lang="text" title="my-tests/data/prices.csv">product,qty,total
apple,2,1.00
melon,3,9.00</cb>
<cb lang="gherkin" title="my-tests/features/pricing-data.feature">Feature: Pricing from a data file

  Background:
    Given the catalogue:
      | product | price |
      | apple   | 0.50  |
      | melon   | 3.00  |

  @data:prices.csv
  Scenario: <qty> x <product> costs <total>
    When I add <qty> "<product>"
    Then the basket total is <total></cb>
<p>Tell bddr where data lives with <code>dataDir: 'data'</code> in the config. Each CSV row becomes a scenario. The steps are the ones you already have. More in <a href="data-files.html">Data files</a>.</p>

<h2>2. A scenario that needs another</h2>
<cb lang="gherkin" title="my-tests/features/orders.feature">@group:orders
Feature: Orders

  @id:create-order
  Scenario: Create an order
    When I place an order for "apple"
    Then the order number is remembered

  @dependsOn:create-order
  Scenario: Cancel the order
    When I cancel the order placed earlier
    Then the order status is "cancelled"</cb>
<cb title="my-tests/steps/orders.steps.ts">import { strict as assert } from 'node:assert';
import { Then, When } from '@bddr/runtime';

When('I place an order for {string}', (ctx, product: string) => {
  ctx.state.set('order', { id: 'ORD-1', product });
});

Then('the order number is remembered', (ctx) => {
  ctx.export('orderId', ctx.state.get<{ id: string }>('order').id);
});

When('I cancel the order placed earlier', (ctx) => {
  const orderId = ctx.from('create-order').get<string>('orderId');
  ctx.state.set('cancelled', orderId);
});

Then('the order status is {string}', (ctx, status: string) => {
  assert.ok(ctx.state.get<string>('cancelled'));
  assert.equal(status, 'cancelled');
});</cb>
<p>"Cancel the order" waits for "Create an order" and reads the order number it exported. See <a href="dependencies.html">Dependencies</a>.</p>

<h2>3. Run in parallel, keep the orders in order</h2>
<cb title="my-tests/bddr.config.ts">import { defineConfig } from '@bddr/cli';

export default defineConfig({
  features: 'features/**/*.feature',
  steps: 'steps/**/*.ts',
  dataDir: 'data',
  workers: 4,
  history: true,
  retry: { scenario: 1 },
  groups: {
    orders: { mode: 'serial' },
  },
  reporters: ['console', ['html', { outputFile: 'reports/report.html' }]],
  profiles: {
    smoke: { tags: '@smoke' },
  },
});</cb>
<ul>
  <li><code>workers: 4</code> runs up to four scenarios at a time.</li>
  <li>The <code>orders</code> group is serial, so its members run one after the other while the other scenarios run beside them.</li>
  <li><code>retry</code> gives a failed scenario one more attempt. A pass after a failure is reported as flaky.</li>
  <li><code>history: true</code> records each run in <code>.bddr/history</code>.</li>
  <li>The <code>smoke</code> profile selects scenarios tagged <code>@smoke</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Look at the plan, then run</h2>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr plan</cb>
<cb lang="text">Plan: 7 scenario(s), 1 group(s), workers=4
  group orders: serial, 2 member(s)

  features/basket.feature::add-a-single-product
  features/basket.feature::totals-for-different-quantities[1]
  features/basket.feature::totals-for-different-quantities[2]
  create-order  [orders]
  features/orders.feature::cancel-the-order  [orders]
      <- create-order
  features/pricing-data.feature::qty-x-product-costs-total[prices.csv#1]
  features/pricing-data.feature::qty-x-product-costs-total[prices.csv#2]</cb>
<p>The arrow shows the dependency. To see why one scenario waits or runs when it does, ask:</p>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr plan --explain create-order</cb>
<p>Then run, with a process per worker, and with only the smoke profile:</p>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr run --pool process
$ npx bddr -p smoke
$ npx bddr validate
$ npx bddr history</cb>
<p><code>validate</code> checks the specs and steps without running them. <code>history</code> becomes useful after a few runs: it lists the flakiest and slowest scenarios.</p>

